  "raw_text": "Step 1: To find which equation has the largest solution \\(x\\), we need to solve each equation for \\(x\\) and compare the values of \\(x\\).\n\nStep 2: For equation (A): \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\), solving for \\(x\\) gives \\(x = \\frac{\\log(\\frac{7}{3})}{\\log(1 + r)}\\).\n\nStep 3: For equation (B): \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\\), solving for \\(x\\) gives \\(x = \\frac{\\log(\\frac{7}{3})}{\\log(1 + \\frac{r}{10})}\\).\n\nStep 4: For equation (C): \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\), solving for \\(x\\) gives \\(x = \\frac{\\log(\\frac{7}{3})}{\\log(1 + 2r)}\\).\n\nStep 5: For equation (D): \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\\), solving for \\(x\\) gives \\(x = \\frac{\\log(\\frac{7}{3})}{\\log(1 + \\sqrt{r})}\\).\n\nStep 6: For equation (E): \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7\\), solving for \\(x\\) gives \\(x = \\frac{\\log(\\frac{7}{3})}{\\log(1 + \\frac{1}{r})}\\).\n\nStep 7: Since \\(0 < r < 3\\), we know that \\(1 + r > 1 + \\frac{r}{10} > 1 + \\sqrt{r} > 1 + 2r > 1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\) because as the base increases, the value of \\(x\\) decreases when the logarithm is taken.\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{E}<|im_end|>",
  "reforward_peak_bytes": 15504216064,
